# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chemlab environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and adjust as needed:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Every variable below is OPTIONAL — the app runs with no env file at all and
# falls back to the defaults shown here. `.env.local` is git-ignored.
#
# Variables come in two kinds.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` are inlined by Next.js into the browser bundle at BUILD time.
# They are visible to anyone who loads the site, so never put a secret behind
# this prefix, and rebuild after changing one — restarting the server is not
# enough.
#
# Everything else is SERVER-ONLY and must never gain that prefix.
# `pnpm bundle:check` greps the built client output for these values and fails
# the build if one appears, because nothing else in the toolchain would say so.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Canonical origin of the deployment. Used for metadataBase, the canonical
# link, Open Graph / Twitter URLs, and the absolute OG image URL.
# Set this to your real domain in production, otherwise social cards and
# canonical URLs point at localhost.
# Must be an absolute URL including the scheme.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Product name shown in page titles, metadata and the UI.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_NAME=Chemlab

# Description used for the meta description and Open Graph / Twitter cards.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_DESCRIPTION="Chemlab is a fun, interactive, and kid-friendly web app to explore chemistry. Learn atoms, molecules, elements, reactions, and take random quizzes—all without an account."

# Optional Twitter/X handle for the twitter:site card tag, including the "@".
# Leave unset to omit the tag entirely.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_HANDLE=@ChemlabApp

# Hosts a lesson image or video may be served from, comma-separated. This is
# an ALLOW-LIST, not a convenience: without it, a pasted URL in a lesson body
# is any host on the internet inside an <img src>. Unset means the built-in
# default below. Public by necessity — the editor validates against it in the
# browser as well as on the server.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_HOSTS=res.cloudinary.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (server-only — NEVER prefix these NEXT_PUBLIC_)
#
# A NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable is inlined into the JavaScript every visitor
# downloads, and a Neon connection string carries its password inline. Setting
# either of these as NEXT_PUBLIC_ publishes your database credentials.
#
# The app runs fine with both unset: `pnpm dev`, `pnpm build` and the test
# suite need no database. Set them when you want to run migrations or query
# real data.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# LOCAL Postgres (the default for development)
#
# The repo ships with a local cluster; no cloud account is needed:
#
#   pnpm db:local:start   # start PostgreSQL on 127.0.0.1:5432
#   pnpm env:check        # confirm what the app will actually connect to
#   pnpm db:migrate       # apply db/migrations
#   pnpm db:seed          # load the JSON content (safe to re-run)
#   pnpm db:check         # prove connectivity and report applied migrations
#   pnpm db:local:reset   # drop everything and start over (local hosts only)
#
# The driver is chosen from the host: a *.neon.tech host uses the Neon
# serverless driver, anything else uses node-postgres over TCP. Locally both
# variables can point at the same URL — there is no connection pooler in
# front of a local cluster, so there is no direct/pooled distinction.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://chemlab:chemlab@127.0.0.1:5432/chemlab
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=postgresql://chemlab:chemlab@127.0.0.1:5432/chemlab

# NOTE ON PRECEDENCE: unlike stock Next.js, `.env.local` OVERRIDES a variable
# that is already set in the shell (see lib/load-env.ts). Hosted dev
# containers sometimes inject a DATABASE_URL of their own, and without this
# the file you just edited would silently do nothing. `.env.local` is
# git-ignored and never deployed, so this cannot affect production.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosted Postgres (Neon) — the deployed environment
#
# SET BOTH. Neon gives you two hosts for the same database, differing only by
# "-pooler" in the hostname, and three things in this repo need the DIRECT one:
#
#   1. Migrations and the seed (`db/seed/connect.ts`). PgBouncer in transaction
#      mode cannot hold the session-level locks DDL needs, so drizzle-kit fails
#      with a lock error that reads like a bug in the migration.
#   2. The admin analytics client (`db/analytics-client.ts`). It sets
#      `statement_timeout` on the CONNECTION, and a transaction pooler may hand
#      the next statement to a different backend where that setting is not in
#      force. This one does not fail — the bound silently stops applying, which
#      is the failure mode the timeout exists to prevent.
#   3. `pnpm db:verify` and the maintenance scripts, for the same reason as (1).
#
# All three fall back to DATABASE_URL when the direct endpoint is unset, so a
# pooled-only configuration looks like it works right up until it does not.
# `pnpm env:check` refuses that combination outright and says why.
#
# Both need `?sslmode=require`; Neon rejects a connection without it and
# reports an SSL error rather than a configuration one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Neon POOLED endpoint — note the "-pooler" in the host. Used by the app.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/chemlab?sslmode=require

# Neon DIRECT endpoint — the SAME endpoint without "-pooler".
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx.region.aws.neon.tech/chemlab?sslmode=require

# After setting them, prove the configuration before deploying:
#
#   pnpm env:check     # refuses a pooled URL with no direct one, and reports
#                      # which endpoint each client will actually use
#   pnpm db:migrate    # applies db/migrations through the direct endpoint
#   pnpm db:seed       # loads the JSON content (safe to re-run)
#   pnpm db:verify     # proves the seeded content matches data/, field by field

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication (server-only — NEVER prefix these NEXT_PUBLIC_)
#
# All four are OPTIONAL. Without them the site serves every public page and the
# account UI simply does not appear — `pnpm env:check` reports which of
# "email/password and Google", "email/password only" or "disabled" you have.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs session cookies and tokens. Generate once and keep it:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Rotating it invalidates every existing session.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# The canonical origin auth runs on. It must match the origin in the Google
# redirect URI EXACTLY, or the callback fails with an error that reads like a
# configuration problem and is one.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth — both halves or neither
#
# The owner creates these in the Google Cloud console; see docs/AUTH.md for the
# exact consent-screen and redirect-URI settings. The redirect URI must be
# registered verbatim as:
#
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
#   https://<production-domain>/api/auth/callback/google
#
# Google accepts no wildcards, so every origin that needs Google sign-in must
# be listed. Put the real values in .env.local or the deployment's environment
# — never in this file, an issue, or a pull request.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The first Super Admin (server-only)
#
# Granting a role requires being a Super Admin, so the first grant cannot
# happen through the app. Sign up normally with this address, then run:
#
#   pnpm db:bootstrap-admin
#
# The script never creates the account — that way the password is hashed by
# Better Auth and never exists in a script, an env var, or a shell history.
# See docs/PERMISSIONS.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Integrations (server-only — NEVER prefix these NEXT_PUBLIC_)
#
# All optional. The admin settings screen reports each of these as
# "Configured" or "Not configured" and NEVER shows a value, a prefix or a
# length — a length alone is enough to tell two candidate keys apart.
#
# The split this file and the settings screen share: environment variables
# hold SECRETS and boot-time infrastructure; the `settings` table holds
# runtime PRODUCT configuration. Nothing that must stay secret has a row.
# See docs/SETTINGS.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Web push (#24). Generate a matched pair with `pnpm vapid:keys`, which prints
# them under exactly these names.
#
# The PUBLIC half carries the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix ON PURPOSE — it is public by
# design, since every subscribing browser is handed it, and the browser cannot
# subscribe without it. This is the one exception to the rule above, and it is
# why the private half is named differently: they are not a matching pair of
# variable names, and that asymmetry is deliberate.
#
# All three are needed. A pair without a subject is a 400 at send time, so
# `pnpm env:check` reports two out of three as "not configured".
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:owner@example.com

# CI alerts (#24). SERVER-ONLY, all three — none may carry NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# CI_NOTIFY_SECRET is the HMAC key: the workflow signs its request body with
# it and the app recomputes the signature. It is never transmitted, which is
# the point of a signature rather than a bearer token — so a copy of it is the
# only way to forge a CI notification. Generate one with
# `openssl rand -hex 32` and set the SAME value as a GitHub Actions secret and
# in the deployment's environment.
#
# CI_NOTIFY_URL is where the workflow posts — always the PRODUCTION
# deployment, so the alert path does not depend on the thing that may be
# broken. It lives in GitHub Actions only; the app never reads it.
# CI_NOTIFY_SECRET=
# CI_NOTIFY_URL=https://example.com/api/ci/notify

# Incoming-webhook URL for operational alerts. The URL *is* the credential:
# anyone holding it can post to the channel. Absent is fine — CI alerts still
# reach Web Push, and the endpoint reports `slack: "skipped"`.
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Cloudinary uploads (#27). All FOUR are needed; three out of four counts as
# not configured, because the fourth failure happens at upload time.
#
# The tables, the signing and the folder convention are in place; the sign and
# confirm endpoints and the upload UI are not, so setting these prepares the
# configuration rather than turning anything on today. `pnpm env:check` and the
# admin settings screen report them so it can be verified in advance.
#
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET is server-only and signs every upload this server
# authorises: a copy of it is the ability to write anything into the account.
# `pnpm bundle:check` greps the built client output for its value.
# CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
# CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=
#
# The environment prefix every upload goes under: one lowercase path segment,
# e.g. `production`, `development`, or `preview-pr-42`. Set it PER DEPLOYMENT
# and never derive it in code from NODE_ENV — a preview runs a production build
# and NODE_ENV reads "production" there, which is exactly how a preview's
# uploads end up in the production folder and its clean-up takes real content
# with them.
# CLOUDINARY_UPLOAD_FOLDER=development

# Outgoing email (verification, digests). Also NOT YET READ — reported for the
# same reason as Cloudinary above.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
